{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"name":"ir","display_name":"R","language":"R"},"language_info":{"name":"R","codemirror_mode":"r","pygments_lexer":"r","mimetype":"text/x-r-source","file_extension":".r","version":"4.4.0"},"kaggle":{"accelerator":"none","dataSources":[],"isInternetEnabled":true,"language":"r","sourceType":"notebook","isGpuEnabled":false}},"nbformat_minor":4,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Github copilote in a Kaggle R-notebook\n\nTo install github copilot on a Kaggle machine we have to go through the following steps:\n\n1. We need to set the `COPILOT_GITHUB_TOKEN` in the environment\n2. We need to install the Unix/Linux command `gh` (github cli client), **and _login_ into github** using the `COPILOT_GITHUB_TOKEN`\n3. We need to install `copilot`\n\nBriefly: `COPILOT_GITHUB_TOKEN` is stored as a Kaggle _Secret_ (in the Add-ons dropdown menu), who's value is a GitHub Fine Grain Token generated on your github.com account. Github CLI client is a program that allows you to manage github from the command line without the use of a browser (here just used for loging in). `copilot` is the program that allows us to chat with an LLM from the command line (and do a whole lot more!).\n\n## Setting the token\n\nA command line interface (CLI) terminal window is very similar to an R console window. It is where you can give the operating system commands, just like in R you can 'run commands' on the console window. You can also write a sequence of commands in a text file that can then be read by the CLI and executed one line at a time (just like a .R script file). In fact the CLI defines a scripting language, which is different from R, that you can use to write function and loops, just like in R. The difference really is that while R is meant to do statistical manipulations and computations on data, the CLI is meant to do manipulations and computations on files and directories in your operating system. \n\nWhile you are probably used to GUI to interact with your computer, most programmers and 'old fashioned' statisticians and researchers prefer the CLI. Being old fashioned still doesn't mean that they don't want to use modern LLM's. So they need a program (aka utility or tool) in the CLI environment that allows them to do that. That utility is `copilot` or `claude` or `codex` or .... However, when you open ChatGPT or Claude in your browser, you need to log in on their website. Similarly for GitHub `copilote` you need to login on github.com. But when you use a CLI how do you do that? Well, you need another program/tool, and that tool is called `gh`. In interactive mode (with direct access to the terminal window) that tool makes login virtually the same as in the browser, however, we often want to automate logging in programmatically. For example you want this notebook to carry out the login for you by talking to the terminal; if this is a multistep process (first login name, then password) this becomes complicated. To make this more convenient, github allows us to use a _athentication token_: a unique secret character sequence that stands in for login + password and that can be passed between computers in each communication. That character sequence is what you can generate yourself in the browser. You can store it on your computer or here in Kaggle in your 'Secrets' vault under the Add-ons dropdown menu. Those tokens can be read from your Kaggle Secrets vault within R. But it doesn't do much good there. The CLI environment to which this notebook talks needs to have access to the token,and so we have to tell the CLI to remember the token in its environment as the value of an CLI environment _variable_ (much like variables in the R console environment). \n\nTelling the environment the token is our first step:","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# 1. Secret -> env var\nSys.setenv(COPILOT_GITHUB_TOKEN = get_user_secret(\"COPILOT_GITHUB_TOKEN\"))","metadata":{"_uuid":"051d70d956493feee0c6d64651c6a088724dca2a","_execution_state":"idle","trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-08-25T15:09:38.31812Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-08-25T15:09:38.319277Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-08-25T15:09:38.612725Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-08-25T15:09:38.608371Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":13},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# test to see if it is defined with the following CLI command (system passes our command to the CLI)\nsystem('test -n \"$COPILOT_GITHUB_TOKEN\" && echo \"Token is set\" || echo \"Token is missing\"', intern=TRUE)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-08-25T15:10:59.856444Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-08-25T15:10:59.857701Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-08-25T15:10:59.870943Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-08-25T15:10:59.869697Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/html":"'Token is set'","text/markdown":"'Token is set'","text/latex":"'Token is set'","text/plain":"[1] \"Token is set\""},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":14},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Loging into GitHub with `gh`\n\nThe github CLI utility is not pre-installed on the Linux system on which this notebook runs. So we need to install it (much like we install and new R library that's missing). We need to do that with a CLI command, so we need `system()` to pass the installation command to the CLI","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# 2. gh from GitHub's own repo (also installs curl/ca-certificates/gpg)\nsystem(\n  paste(\n    \"set -e;\",\n    \"apt-get update -qq;\",\n    \"apt-get install -y -qq curl ca-certificates gpg;\",\n    \"mkdir -p -m 755 /etc/apt/keyrings;\",\n    \"curl -fsSL https://cli.github.com/packages/githubcli-archive-keyring.gpg\",\n    \"| tee /etc/apt/keyrings/githubcli-archive-keyring.gpg > /dev/null;\",\n    \"chmod go+r /etc/apt/keyrings/githubcli-archive-keyring.gpg;\",\n    'echo \"deb [arch=$(dpkg --print-architecture) signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/githubcli-archive-keyring.gpg] https://cli.github.com/packages stable main\"',\n    \"| tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/github-cli.list > /dev/null;\",\n    \"apt-get update -qq;\",\n    \"apt-get install -y gh;\",\n    \"gh --version\"\n  ), intern=TRUE\n) |> cat(sep=\"\\n\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-08-25T15:13:08.051525Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-08-25T15:13:08.052754Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-08-25T15:13:16.611894Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-08-25T15:13:16.610625Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Reading package lists...\nBuilding dependency tree...\nReading state information...\ngh is already the newest version (2.98.0).\n0 upgraded, 0 newly installed, 0 to remove and 268 not upgraded.\ngh version 2.98.0 (2026-08-20)\nhttps://github.com/cli/cli/releases/tag/v2.98.0\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":15},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Now we can login using `gh` but we need to tell it that instead of using a login and password, we'll use the secret `COPILOT_GITHUB_TOKEN` (we also specify the hostname, which is github.com and the communication protocol which is encrypted http:","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# 3. Authenticate\nsystem('printf \"%s\" \"$COPILOT_GITHUB_TOKEN\" | gh auth login --hostname github.com --with-token --git-protocol https 2>&1', intern = TRUE) |> \n  cat(\"\\n\",sep=\"\\n\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-08-25T15:15:56.941307Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-08-25T15:15:56.942538Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-08-25T15:15:57.721341Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-08-25T15:15:57.719972Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"\n\n\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":16},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# check to see if we're logged in\nsystem('gh auth status', intern=TRUE) |> cat(sep=\"\\n\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-08-25T15:16:14.977835Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-08-25T15:16:14.978988Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-08-25T15:16:15.421448Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-08-25T15:16:15.42002Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"github.com\n  ✓ Logged in to github.com account raoelg (/root/.config/gh/hosts.yml)\n  - Active account: true\n  - Git operations protocol: https\n  - Token: github_pat_**********************************************************************************\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":17},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Installing `copilot`\n\nThe `copilot` tool is also not pre-installed on this Linux CLI, so we need to install it. The documentation tells us that we need to use a specific package installer, called `npm`, to install it. Now `npm` is usually pre-installed on Linux CLI's but not on the one used for Kaggle notebooks. So we first need to install `npm`, and then use `npm` to install the program that we really want which is `copilot`. \n\nTo make sure there are no conflicting versions or files used by `npm` we first instruct the CLI to purge any files associatedd with `npm`. Then we download the `npm` source code and install `npm` and necessary files.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# 4. Node 22\nsystem(\"apt-get purge -y libnode-dev nodejs npm && apt-get autoremove -y \",intern=FALSE) \nsystem(\"curl -fsSL https://deb.nodesource.com/setup_22.x | bash - && apt-get install -y nodejs && node --version\",intern=TRUE) |> cat(sep=\"\\n\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-08-25T15:23:03.691874Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-08-25T15:23:03.692984Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-08-25T15:23:21.891021Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-08-25T15:23:21.889318Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"\u001b[38;5;79m2026-08-25 15:23:08 - Installing pre-requisites\u001b[0m\nHit:1 https://deb.nodesource.com/node_22.x nodistro InRelease\nHit:2 https://cli.github.com/packages stable InRelease\nHit:3 http://archive.ubuntu.com/ubuntu jammy InRelease\nHit:4 http://security.ubuntu.com/ubuntu jammy-security InRelease\nHit:5 http:/